Forward Declaration of Coverpoints and Crosses in Covergroups
This feature enables forward declaration of coverpoints and crosses within a covergroup.
It allows you to reference coverpoints or crosses as cross items before their actual
declaration in the covergroup body.
To enable forward declaration of coverpoints and crosses within a covergroup, use the
-xlrm cg_forward_decl compile-time option.
% vcs -xlrm cg_forward_decl
In the following example, coverpoints cp1 and cp2 are used to define the cross cr1 even
before they are declared:
Example 37 Forward Declaration of Coverpoints and Crosses
int x;
int y;
cr1: cross cp1, cp2;
cp1: coverpoint x;
cp2: coverpoint y;

Support for CrossQueueType and CrossValType
VCS supports CrossQueueType and CrossValType as defined in the SystemVerilog LRM.
The definition of CrossValType is a SystemVerilog struct consisting of one member for
each coverpoint in the cross. The definition of CrossQueueType is an unbounded queue
of CrossValType elements. CrossQueueType and CrossValType enable you to focus
on required cross bins by defining queues for tuples or by defining functions returning
queues. They also provide ease-of-use in defining ignore and illegal bins.
As defined in the SystemVerilog LRM, VCS provides automatically defined types,
CrossQueueType and CrossValType, for tuples and queues in each cross. This feature is
enabled by default, therefore, no additional switch is required at compile time.
Consider the following example that finds matching bin tuples with the value tuples defined
in the CrossQueueType construct. These bin tuples are then used to sample the cross
bins.
Example 38 Cross cr1
int x, y;

covergroup cg (int limit) @(posedge clk) ;
cp1: coverpoint x {
bins b1 = {[1:10]};
bins b2 = {[5:15]};
}
cp2: coverpoint y {
bins c1 = {[30:60]};
bins c2 = {[70:100]};
}
cr1: cross cp1, cp2 {
bins r2 = return_tuple(limit);

function CrossQueueType return_tuple(int limit);
for(int i=1; i<limit; i++)
return_tuple.push_back('{i*limit, i*limit*20});
}
cg cg1=new(2);

always #9 clk = ~clk;

