Modeling User-Defined Primitives
User-Defined Primitives (UDPs) have multiple input ports and exactly one output port.
Each UDP has exactly one output, which can be modeled in one of three states: 0, 1, or x.
The three-state value, z, is not supported.
There are the following two types of UDPs:
• Combinational UDP: This UDP uses the value of its inputs to determine the next value
of its output. If the value of its inputs is constant 1, the output of a combinational UDP
is determined as constant 1. A combinational UDP has one field per input and one field
for the output. The input fields are separated from the output field by a colon (:), as
shown here:
<input1> <input2> <input3>: <output>; 0 1 0 : 1;
• Sequential UDP: This UDP uses the value of its inputs and the current value of its
output to determine the value of its output. A sequential UDP has one field per input
and one field for the output like a combinational UDP along with one additional field, as
shown here:
<input1> <input2> <input3>: <additional_field> : <output>; 0 1 0 : 0
: 1;
where, <additional_field> represents the current state of the UDP

Each UDP is analyzed line-by-line to check whether the output can be a constant value.
Each line is matched against the known constants in its list of input ports. If all lines
produce the same constant value, the output is considered a constant value.
Consider the following UDP, mux_2_to_1, in which mux is the output port, s0 and s1 are
select inputs, and i0 to i3 are input ports:
