Measuring Line Coverage Metric
This section discusses how to measure the line coverage metric in your verification plan.
The section consists of the following subsections:
• Specifying Pragmas
• Finding the Named Objects
• Use Model
Specifying Pragmas
Pragmas are special comments in a design source code. The
coverage name pragma
"coverage name " pragma
is used to assign names to line coverage objects so that they are referenced from your
verification plan. The "coverage name " pragma syntax is as follows:
// coverage name <object name>
Finding the Named Objects
Case 1
Consider the following design source code:
//`timescale 100ms /10ms

module test(clk);
input clk;
reg a, b, c;

reg [3:0] z, w, x, y, bob;

initial
a = 1'b0;
b = 1'b1;
//coverage name testline1
if (a && b && clk) c = 1'b0;
a = 1'b1;

x <= w & z;
y <= w[3:0] & z[3:0];

// coverage name testline2
bob <= w & 3'b1;
end
endmodule // test

initial begin
// coverage name topline1
clk = 1'b0;
forever begin
#(50)
// coverage name topline2
clk = ~clk;
end
end
initial begin
repeat (100) @ (posedge clk);
$finish;
end

test inst1(clk);
test inst2(clk);

endmodule // top
where, the test module has instances, top.inst1and top.inst2. The named objects
in the test module are testline1 and testline2, and the named objects in the top
module are topline1 and topline2. To find the named objects for measuring the metrics,
you can specify the following in the HVP plan accordingly:
• To find all the named objects in the top module, specify the following:
source = "module: top@**";
// find all named objects in module top for the measured // metric(s)
You can find the following list of matching objects in the top module:
"top.topline1"
"top.topline2"

• To find the object by name testline1 in the entire design source code, specify the
source = "instance: **@testline1";
// find named object testline1 in full design for the
// measured metric(s)
You can find the following list of matching named objects with testline1 as the object
name in all the instances of the entire design source code:
"top.inst1.testline1"
"top.inst2.testline1"
• To find all the named objects in the entire design, specify the following:
source = "instance: **@**";
// find all named objects in full design for the measured
// metric(s)
You can find the following list of matching objects in all the instances of the entire
design source code:
"top.topline1"
"top.topline2"
"top.inst1.testline1"
"top.inst1.testline2"
"top.inst2.testline1"
"top.inst2.testline2"
• To find all the named objects in all the modules starting with t, specify the following:
source = "module: t*@*"
// find all named objects in module t* for the measured
// metric(s)
You can find the following list of matching objects in all the modules starting with t:
"top.topline1"
"top.topline2"
"test.testline1"
"test.testline2"
• To find the named object testline1 in all the instances under top, specify the
source = "instance: top.*@testline1"
// find instance "top.*" and find named object testline1 // for the
measured metric(s)

You can find the following list of matching objects in the top.* instance with
testline1 as the object name:
"top.inst1.testline1"
"top.inst2.testline1"
• To find all the top.* instances and with the named object topline1, specify the
source = "instance: top.*@topline1"
// find instance "top.*" and find named object topline1
You can find that there is no matching object in the top.* instance with topline1 as
the object name:
No matching region.
To measure the line coverage metric, define the object name in the design source code
using pragmas as shown in the following example:
module test;

case(oper)
3'b000:
// coverage name test_000
logic_out=4'bxxxx;
$display("case has value 000");
end

3'b001:
if(op)
logic_out=~din1&din2;
// coverage name test_001
$display(" extra line added");
end

3'b010:
case(op)
// coverage name test_010
0: logic_out= ~(din1&din2);
1: $display("case is 010");
endcase
end

Following is the HVP plan used to measure the coverage of the named lines in the
preceding example:
plan myPlan;
measure Line, Toggle module;
source = "module: logic_block_tb@**";
source = "module: top@**";
feature Feature_11;
measure Line, Cond, Toggle, Branch mod_2;
source = "module: logical_block_dut@*";
feature Feature_2;
measure Line, Cond, FSM, Toggle, Branch, Assert, test,
AssertResult Inst_1;
source = "instance : test_top.top1.**.**@test*";
feature Feature_3;
measure Line, FSM, Toggle, Branch Measure_1;
source = "tree: test_top.top1.tb1";
After annotating the scores and generating the URG report, you are able to see the line
coverage details for the test_top instance.
