Compiling the C Code
After writing the C code for the subprogram, you must compile the code into a shared
object library. See the Compiling the C Code in the Foreign Architectures section.
Foreign Applications
Creating a foreign application involves the following steps:
1. Writing the C code for the application
2. Compiling the C code into a shared object library
Then you can load or integrate the foreign application into a runtime environment.
Writing the C Code for a Foreign Application
The C code of the application uses VHPI functions to access and manipulate the simulator
data structures and interact with the simulation environment. The application must have an
entry point function that is called by the simulator after dynamically loading the application
library. The entry point function is called after elaboration. A typical use of the entry point
function is to traverse the hierarchy of the design, get and set values for objects, register
value-change callbacks, and so on. See the Static Design Access and VHPI Callbacks
sections for more information about using the VHPI functions.

Foreign Applications
Compiling the C Code
After writing the C code for the application, you must compile the code into a shared object
library. See the Compiling the C Code in the Foreign Architectures section.
Loading a Foreign Application into the Runtime Environment
You can load the foreign application by invoking the simulator with the -vhpi option as
follows:
simv [options] -vhpi
<library_name>:<entry_point_function_name>:<model_name>
<library_name>: Specifies the platform independent name of the foreign application
you want to load. It is translated into a platform-specific name by the simulator when
it loads the application. For example, on Solaris the string “LibName” is translated to
“libLibName.so”.
<entry_point_function_name>: Specifies the name of the C function in the user-created
shared object library which is the entry-point to the application, that is, the function which
gets called by simv after the application is loaded.
<model_name>: Specifies a unique name associated with the application, which simv
uses for printing informative and error messages during elaboration and simulation.
You can load multiple foreign applications by specifying the -vhpi option
multiple times with appropriate trailing arguments. The applications are loaded
in no specific order.

VHPI Version Compatibility and Utility Functions
The VHPI information model contains class of type tool. You can query the VHPI version,
the VHDL compliance level, the VHPI compliance level, and the last release of the
simulator that this version of VHPI is not compatible with.
For example, to get a handle for a tool class type, you can use the vhpiTool relationship
from a NULL handle. To get the last release of the simulator that this version of VHPI is
not compatible with, you use the vhpiIncompatibleVersionP string property from that
handle.
vhpiHandleT tool;

tool = vhpi_handle(vhpiTool, NULL);
vhpi_printf("This VHPI is not compatible with the release
%s\n", vhpi_get_str(vhpiIncompatibleVersionP, tool));

vhpi_release_handle(tool);
generates the following output:
This VHPI is not compatible with the release 2000.02
